{"agent_result": {"cost_usd": 0.03694401, "exit_code": 0, "final_message": "The controller satisfies the task. The official run with `sim.py` finishes at (-0.949, 0.541), inside the final box at (-0.90, 0.55). The spec verdict passes: `waypoint_one`, `waypoint_two` and `waypoint_three_and_safe` all pass, with a minimum robustness margin of about 0.17. There were no errors and no box contact; the collision spec holds.\n\nI also ran the same controller over the perturbation grid from `task.yaml` (latency 0 or 100 ms, friction scale 0.85 or 1.15). All four combinations pass with similar margins. I did not test the observation-noise perturbation separately.\n\n**What `controller.py` does**\n- **Waypoints:** It visits (0.35, 0.55), then (-0.20, 0.55), then (-0.90, 0.55). It advances when the robot is within 0.08 m or has crossed the waypoint's perpendicular, which prevents overshoot loops.\n- **Heading:** A PD loop on `heading_trim` turns the robot toward the next waypoint. It turns in place when the heading error exceeds 0.8 rad.\n- **Speed:** The wheel commands are torques, so I close a PI loop on the wheel spin rates to set a cruise speed of 0.5 m/s. It slows in proportion to remaining distance.\n- **Final stop:** Within 0.10 m of the last waypoint, it brakes to a stop and holds the arrival heading.\n\nThe earlier versions failed for two reasons. Open-loop drive overshot the waypoints. Aggressive heading gains spun the yaw hinge into its \u00b16.4 rad limit and pushed the robot out of the arena. The current gains are chosen to avoid both.\n\nI left `sim.py`'s run outputs in `.qddot/` in the task folder, since that is where the script writes them. My scratch probes are in `/tmp/qddot_scratch`. I did not open the reference files in the task folder (`expected.yaml`, `controllers/`) and did not use them.", "tokens_in": 34, "tokens_out": 34671, "transcript_path": "/var/folders/t8/v6739bx57tq_lp9kw4qz1gyr0000gn/T/qddot-bench-z2arj3y9/transcript.txt", "wall_s": 175.756727874